    "citations": [
      "1756",
      "1732"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "The effects of rewetting on plant species richness and diversity are mixed, with some cases showing no effect or a reduction in diversity.",
    "citations": [
      "1756",
      "1732"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "When combined with tree removal, rewetting increases the presence of wetland-characteristic plants.",
    "citations": [
      "1732"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "When combined with tree removal, rewetting increases herb cover.",
    "citations": [
      "1732"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "When combined with tree removal, rewetting reduces forest-characteristic vegetation.",
    "citations": [
      "1732"
    ]
  }
]

[
  {
    "statement": "There are key differences between completely excluding livestock versus reducing grazing intensity on degraded peatlands.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Multiple studies have evaluated livestock exclusion from degraded peatlands.",
    "citations": [
      "1734"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "The evidence shows mixed but generally limited effects for livestock exclusion from degraded peatlands.",
    "citations": [
      "1734"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Most studies found livestock exclusion had no effect on herb cover, including cottongrass and sedges.",
    "citations": [
      "1734"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Livestock exclusion has no consistent effect on Sphagnum moss cover.",
    "citations": [
      "1734"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "In many cases, livestock exclusion had no effect on shrub cover, though some studies showed increased shrub cover.",
    "citations": [
      "1734"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "One study found that livestock exclusion increased vegetation biomass.",
    "citations": [
      "1734"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "The effects of livestock exclusion on vegetation biomass varied between wetter and drier areas of bogs.",
    "citations": [
      "1734"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Only one study has evaluated the approach of reducing grazing intensity specifically in bogs.",
    "citations": [
      "1735"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "A study found that lower grazing intensity was associated with greater total vegetation cover.",
    "citations": [
      "1735"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "A study found that lower grazing intensity was associated with greater shrub cover.",
    "citations": [
      "1735"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "A study found that lower grazing intensity was associated with greater cottongrass cover for one species.",
    "citations": [
      "1735"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "A study found that lower grazing intensity was associated with higher vegetation biomass.",
    "citations": [
      "1735"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Based on limited evidence, reducing grazing intensity shows more consistent positive effects on vegetation cover and biomass compared to complete livestock exclusion.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Complete livestock exclusion shows more variable results, with many studies finding no significant effects on key peatland vegetation components.",
    "citations": []
  }
]

[
  {
